From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 19:10:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] renderer: the planes of an enhancement layer can have upscalers
 of their own

The planes of a Dolby Vision enhancement layer (profile 7) are scaled up
to the size of the base layer with the filter of the chroma of the base
layer: plane_upscaler, or upscaler where that is not set. A caller that
has to draw such a picture in less GPU time, where the GPU cannot keep up
with the video otherwise, can only change the filter of all three kinds
of planes at once: the chroma of the base layer, and the luma and the
chroma of the enhancement layer, which do not cost the same and do not
change the picture as much.

New pl_render_params.el_luma_upscaler and el_chroma_upscaler: the
upscaler of the plane of the enhancement layer that carries luma, and of
those that carry only chroma, told by their component mapping, as the
sampler of the layer reads them. Where one is set, that plane is sampled
as before with it in place of plane_upscaler, from a copy of the
parameters for that call. The planes of the base layer, and the scaling
down of any plane, are not affected. Both NULL, the default: no copy is
made and nothing changes, not even the text of a shader.

Measured with Kodi's Dolby Vision renderer (tools/dvbridge in the Kodi of
LibreELEC Hades) on a software OpenGL ES (zink on lavapipe), three
pictures of a profile 7 FEL stream, drawn the two ways the renderer has:
with both NULL, the reconstructed picture and the transport are bit for
bit those without this patch. With the chroma of the base layer, then
also the chroma, then also the luma of the enhancement layer scaled up
bilinearly, the reconstructed picture is the one that a test-only change
choosing the filter of each kind of plane gave, in every pixel.

Measured again with libplacebo-9913 and 9914 applied first, which this
patch follows: with both NULL, the same bytes as with those two alone;
with the filters changed one kind at a time, the same pictures as the
test-only change, in every pixel, once that change also picks the filter
where 9914 asks how the other planes are scaled. Once the chroma of the
base layer is scaled up bilinearly, no plane of the base layer has a
scaler that fixes the size of its shader, which the texel-exact read of
a plane that needs no scaling (libplacebo-9914) requires: the luma of
the base layer is then read as before 9914, with linear filtering at the
centres of its texels. That gives the texels themselves where the GPU
computes the weights of the filter in fixed point; on the software
renderer, which computes them in float, it differs from the exact read
by one code of 4095 in 1166 to 1389 of the 8294400 pixels of a picture
(under 0.02 %).

License: LGPL-2.1-or-later, that of libplacebo and of the files changed,
whose notices stay as they are.
---
 src/include/libplacebo/renderer.h | 13 ++++++++++++
 src/renderer.c                    | 33 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++

diff --git a/src/include/libplacebo/renderer.h b/src/include/libplacebo/renderer.h
--- a/src/include/libplacebo/renderer.h
+++ b/src/include/libplacebo/renderer.h
@@ -151,6 +151,19 @@ struct pl_render_params {
     const struct pl_filter_config *plane_upscaler;
     const struct pl_filter_config *plane_downscaler;
 
+    // If set, these override `plane_upscaler` (or `upscaler`, where that is
+    // NULL) for the planes of an enhancement layer (`pl_frame.enhancement_layer`):
+    // `el_luma_upscaler` for a plane that carries luma, `el_chroma_upscaler`
+    // for one that carries only chroma. They are used where such a plane is
+    // scaled up to the size of the base layer; the planes of the base layer,
+    // and the scaling down of any plane, are not affected. Note that a value
+    // of NULL simply means "no override".
+    //
+    // For a caller that trades the precision of the enhancement layer for
+    // speed apart from that of the chroma of the base layer.
+    const struct pl_filter_config *el_luma_upscaler;
+    const struct pl_filter_config *el_chroma_upscaler;
+
     // The anti-ringing strength to apply to filters. See the equivalent option
     // in `pl_sample_filter_params` for more information.
     float antiringing_strength;
diff --git a/src/renderer.c b/src/renderer.c
--- a/src/renderer.c
+++ b/src/renderer.c
@@ -1675,6 +1675,27 @@ static bool want_merge(struct pass_state *pass,
 static int frame_ref(const struct pl_frame *frame);
 
 #ifdef PL_HAVE_DOVI
+// The upscaler that `pl_render_params.el_luma_upscaler` or `el_chroma_upscaler`
+// sets for a plane of an enhancement layer, or NULL. A plane is told by the
+// components it carries, as sample_el() reads them: luma, or only chroma.
+static const struct pl_filter_config *el_plane_upscaler(const struct pl_render_params *params,
+                                                        const struct pl_plane *plane)
+{
+    bool luma = false, chroma = false;
+    for (int c = 0; c < plane->components; c++) {
+        switch (plane->component_mapping[c]) {
+        case PL_CHANNEL_Y: luma = true; continue;
+        case PL_CHANNEL_CB:
+        case PL_CHANNEL_CR: chroma = true; continue;
+        default: continue;
+        }
+    }
+
+    if (luma)
+        return params->el_luma_upscaler;
+    return chroma ? params->el_chroma_upscaler : NULL;
+}
+
 // Build a sub-shader that samples the enhancement layer planes and returns
 // the (color-repr-normalized) EL signal.
 static pl_shader sample_el(struct pass_state *pass, const struct pl_frame *el)
@@ -1759,9 +1780,21 @@ static pl_shader sample_el(struct pass_state *pass, const struct pl_frame *el)
                  ref_tex->params.w, ref_tex->params.h, sx, sy,
                  src.rect.x0, src.rect.y0, src.rect.x1, src.rect.y1);
 
+        // A plane with an upscaler of its own is sampled like the others,
+        // with that one in place of `plane_upscaler`
+        const struct pl_render_params *params = pass->params;
+        const struct pl_filter_config *upscaler = el_plane_upscaler(params, plane);
+        struct pl_render_params el_params;
+        if (upscaler) {
+            el_params = *params;
+            el_params.plane_upscaler = upscaler;
+            pass->params = &el_params;
+        }
+
         pl_shader plane_sh = pl_dispatch_begin_ex(rr->dp, true);
         dispatch_sampler(pass, plane_sh, &rr->samplers_el[i], SAMPLER_PLANE,
                          NULL, &src);
+        pass->params = params;
 
         ident_t sub = sh_subpass(sh, plane_sh);
         pl_dispatch_abort(rr->dp, &plane_sh);
